# Rust performance directives

This project is a Bitcoin blockchain indexer. Ingest throughput is the
product; every code change should be evaluated against it. Default to
performance unless there is a concrete correctness or maintainability reason
to do otherwise. When in doubt, benchmark with `bidx bench` before and after
you change something on the hot path.

## Never

- Never allocate per-byte or per-item on the parse hot path. Reuse buffers,
  or read directly into a fixed-size array. A `Vec::with_capacity` outside a
  loop is cheap; a fresh `Vec::new()` in a tight loop is not.
- Never `format!` / `to_string()` on the hot path. Skip logging in loops
  unless gated behind `tracing::enabled!`.
- Never use `HashMap` / `HashSet` with the default `std` hasher
  (`SipHash`). Use `rustc_hash::FxHashMap` for in-memory, non-persistent
  lookups — we already have `rustc-hash` as a workspace dep.
- Never hold a `Mutex` across `.await` or while doing I/O. Compute first,
  lock only to swap/append results. We use `parking_lot::Mutex` for the UTXO
  pending-table for this reason.
- Never copy a slice you only need to read. Pass `&[u8]`; only `Arc<[u8]>`
  when the data must outlive the borrow (see `ThreadFileCache`).
- Never convert `Hash32` to a hex string on any hot path. Use `to_hex()` only
  for logging/debugging/CLI output. Storage and comparison are always the
  32 raw bytes.
- Never call `.unwrap()` or `.expect()` in production code paths. Only in
  tests. Use `?` with a contextual `anyhow::Context`.

## Prefer

- Prefer `u32`/`u64` arithmetic to byte-order gymnastics; convert once at
  the parse boundary with `u32::from_le_bytes` / `u64::from_le_bytes`.
- Prefer struct-of-arrays / columnar building (see
  `bidx_store::parquet_writers::Col`) over row-by-row pushes when batching
  for Parquet — the whole point of IntermediateFiles is per-column reuse.
- Prefer a bounded `crossbeam_channel` to an unbounded one on any pipeline
  stage; unbounded channels are memory-leaks under backpressure.
- Prefer `Rayon::par_iter` over manual threads for the parse workers; the
  per-thread `ThreadFileCache` pattern in `pipeline.rs` is the shape to copy
  (per-thread init via `try_for_each_init`, lazy file opens, no shared
  state).
- Prefer `assert_eq!` / explicit comparison of fixed-size arrays to
  capturing and comparing formatted strings in tests. Use
  `assert!(left == right, "msg: {:?}", ...)` with raw bytes.
- Prefer `Iterator` adaptors to `for` loops when the result is consumed
  once. Do not combine `.collect::<Vec<_>>` + `sort` when
  `.sort_unstable_by_key` on an existing Vec will do.
- Prefer `[u8; 32]` / `[u8; 36]` fixed-size outpoint/txid keys over `Vec<u8>`
  or `Box<[u8]>`. They are `Copy` and go on the stack.

## RocksDB / storage

- Writes into `UtxoStore` must go through `WriteBatch`, not per-key `put_cf`.
  Undo records must be written atomically with the batch that mutates the
  UTXO set — never write the UTXO change first and the undo second.
- Never open two `UtxoStore` instances to the same path. RocksDB does not
  allow it and will lock the DB.
- RocksDB write batches in the sequential consumer must be as large as
  possible within one block — the whole point of the ordered consumer is
  that each block is one `WriteBatch`. Never split per-transaction.
- `live` mode enables the WAL for durability; `parse` mode uses bulk-load
  settings (no WAL). The disk-space checkpoint measurement
  (`DiskGuardConfig.current_total_bytes`) always includes the WAL, so never
  keep a WAL file outside the measured dir.

## Parquet / Arrow

- Batch as large as the configured `batch_size` — the consumer keeps
  building across blocks until the batch is full. Never force-flush per
  block; that defeats zstd compression and row-group size targets.
- Parquet write batches must be consumed column-by-column (see the `Col`
  type-erased builder in `parquet_writers.rs`). Do not allocate per-row
  Arrow arrays for the normal path.
- New columns go at the END of a table's column list; never reorder
  existing columns (the Parquet schema is positional and the ClickHouse
  DDL maps by name).
- Hashes in Parquet are `FixedSizeBinary(32)`; never `Utf8` hex strings.

## CLI / network / general

- Buffer all file/network writes. Anything that appends to a file in a
  loop should take a `&mut impl Write` or use a `BufWriter`.
- For ZMQ / RPC in `bidx-live`: block the minimum possible on the
  receive side; do the catch_up work after receiving, not while holding
  the receive handle. Keep ZMQ subscriptions to the single
  `hashblock` topic — we already idempotently re-run `catch_up` so extra
  topics buy nothing.
- `logs` on the hot path go through `tracing` with structured fields and
  are only emitted at `info` or coarser; use `debug!` for per-block
  details behind a feature flag. Never `println!` in library code.

## What "done" looks like for perf changes

- For changes touching `bidx-parser`, `bidx-store`, the `pipeline` module,
  `ThreadFileCache`, or the `consume_ordered` consumer thread: run
  `bidx bench --mode full --blocks-dir <test blocks>` before and after
  your change and confirm no throughput regression. Record before/after in
  the PR/commit message if the difference is >2%.
